import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { href } from "../../lib/site.js"
import { MenuHero } from "../../demos/menu/hero.rx"
import { MenuOptions } from "../../demos/menu/options.rx"
import { MenuSubmenu } from "../../demos/menu/submenu.rx"
import { MenuServer } from "../../demos/menu/server.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Menu" subtitle="A list of actions in a popup, opened from a button and driven from the keyboard.">
    <Demo source="menu/hero"><MenuHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="menu/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      The popup is an element with <code>popover="auto"</code>, and the trigger is a button with{" "}
      <code>popovertarget</code>. The browser provides:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The top layer: the popup is drawn above everything else, without a portal or a <code>z-index</code>.</li>
      <li>Closing on <kbd>Escape</kbd> and on a press outside the popup.</li>
      <li>
        Nesting: a submenu's popup is declared inside its parent's, so the browser keeps the parent open while the
        submenu is open, closes the submenu with its parent, and closes only the innermost menu on{" "}
        <kbd>Escape</kbd>.
      </li>
      <li>Placement against the trigger with CSS anchor positioning, flipped to the other side when it doesn't fit.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The platform has no menu keyboard model, so the library adds it: arrow keys that move between items and
      wrap, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd>, typeahead, activation with <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Space</kbd>,
      focus moving into the menu when it opens and back to the trigger when it closes (including after{" "}
      <kbd>Escape</kbd> closes a submenu, which no engine does), the item kinds, submenus that open on hover, the
      ARIA roles, and <code>data-side</code> with the arrow's position.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        The popup stays in the document while it is closed, so there is no Portal part. There is no Positioner or
        Backdrop part either: the popup is the positioned element, and <code>::backdrop</code> styles what is behind
        it. The trigger has no <code>openOnHover</code> (a menu opens on hover only in a{" "}
        <a href={href("components/menubar")}>menubar</a>), and there are no detached triggers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="keyboard">Opening it</h3>
    <p>
      A press on the trigger opens the menu and focuses the popup, so the next arrow key lands on an item.{" "}
      <kbd>Enter</kbd>, <kbd>Space</kbd> and <kbd>ArrowDown</kbd> on the trigger open it on the first item, and{" "}
      <kbd>ArrowUp</kbd> on the last. Choosing an item closes the menu and returns focus to the trigger;{" "}
      <kbd>Tab</kbd> closes it and moves on. Disabled items are skipped by the arrow keys and can't be chosen.
    </p>

    <h3 id="checkbox-and-radio-items">Checkbox and radio items</h3>
    <p>
      <code>MenuCheckboxItem</code> toggles a boolean, and <code>MenuRadioItem</code>s inside a{" "}
      <code>MenuRadioGroup</code> choose one value; both work controlled or not. They carry{" "}
      <code>data-checked</code> or <code>data-unchecked</code>, so the indicator is any child you show for one of
      them. Choosing one leaves the menu open, so several can be set at once. Wrap related items in a{" "}
      <code>MenuGroup</code> with a <code>MenuGroupLabel</code> to name them.
    </p>
    <Demo source="menu/options"><MenuOptions /></Demo>

    <h3 id="close-on-click">Closing on click</h3>
    <p>
      Every item kind takes <code>closeOnClick</code>. A plain <code>MenuItem</code> closes the menu by default,
      checkbox and radio items keep it open, and a <code>MenuLinkItem</code> keeps it open too, since following the
      link usually changes the page anyway.
    </p>

    <h3 id="submenu">Submenus</h3>
    <p>
      A <code>MenuSubmenuRoot</code> holds a <code>MenuSubmenuTrigger</code>, which is an item of the parent menu,
      and the submenu's own <code>MenuPopup</code>, declared inside the parent's popup. Resting the pointer on the
      trigger opens the submenu after <code>delay</code> milliseconds, and the pointer may cross other items on its
      way there without closing it. A press, <kbd>ArrowRight</kbd>, <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Space</kbd> open it
      too, and the keys move focus into it; <kbd>ArrowLeft</kbd> closes it again. Choosing an item in a submenu
      closes the whole tree.
    </p>
    <Demo source="menu/submenu"><MenuSubmenu /></Demo>

    <h3 id="links">Links</h3>
    <p>
      <code>MenuLinkItem</code> renders an <code>{"<a>"}</code> that is a menu item: give it an{" "}
      <code>href</code>, and <kbd>Enter</kbd> follows it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="server">Items from a server</h3>
    <p>
      Load the items in an equation and render its answer inside the popup:{" "}
      <code>{"recent = wanted ? await loadRecent() : []"}</code>, with <code>onOpenChange</code> setting{" "}
      <code>wanted</code> the first time the menu opens. The menu opens at once, whatever your handlers wait for: the
      root calls <code>onOpenChange</code> (and an item's <code>onCheckedChange</code> or a radio group's{" "}
      <code>onValueChange</code>) in a change of its own, so a request it starts never holds the popup, the
      highlight or the tick. Show a fallback with <code>loading on(wanted)</code> around the items; the menu stays
      closable with <kbd>Escape</kbd> and a press outside while it shows.
    </p>
    <p>
      Opened from the keyboard, focus waits on the popup and moves to the first item when the items arrive. Items
      may be replaced while the menu is open (a reload, an error in their place, a retry): if the focused item goes,
      focus moves to the first item rather than leaving the menu. Put an <code>errored</code> region around the items
      with a Retry item (<code>closeOnClick={"{false}"}</code>) that calls <code>refresh</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      An item whose <code>onClick</code> starts a save closes the menu at once: the item closes it in a change of
      its own, so the menu doesn't wait for the request. Show the request's progress outside the menu, here with{" "}
      <code>affects opened</code> and <code>pending? opened</code>. Press the button under the demo to drop its
      network for two seconds and see the error and Retry.
    </p>
    <Demo source="menu/server"><MenuServer /></Demo>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>open</code> and <code>onOpenChange</code> to <code>MenuRoot</code> to hold the state yourself.
      Escape and a press outside can't be refused: they are reported through <code>onOpenChange</code> after the
      browser closed the menu, and a menu whose <code>open</code> stays true is shown again. The element follows
      your <code>open</code> both ways: a handler that writes it only once a request has answered opens or closes the
      menu then, and a <code>guess</code> shows at once and is undone if the request fails. A{" "}
      <code>disabled</code> menu doesn't open.
    </p>

    <h3 id="placement">Placement and the arrow</h3>
    <p>
      The popup sits below its trigger with their start edges lined up; a submenu sits to the right of its
      trigger. <code>side</code>, <code>align</code>, <code>sideOffset</code> and <code>alignOffset</code> become
      CSS anchor positioning declarations in the popup's inline style. When the browser flips the popup, its{" "}
      <code>data-side</code> changes to the side it ended up on. <code>MenuArrow</code> is placed on the edge
      facing the trigger, centred on the trigger, as in the first demo.
    </p>

    <h3 id="animation">Animating it</h3>
    <p>
      Animate the popup in with <code>@starting-style</code> under <code>:popover-open</code>. A press outside
      closes a popover at once, so animating it out is left to CSS: transition <code>display</code> and{" "}
      <code>overlay</code> with <code>allow-discrete</code>, which takes effect in the engines that support it and
      is ignored in the others. The highlighted item has no native selector; style{" "}
      <code>[data-highlighted]</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="menu" />
  </DocsPage>
}
